     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is this a good offer?\n\n**It is a solid, above-average initial offer for Google L6 PM, but it is not a \"blowout\" package that justifies walking away from a role you enjoy at Meta.**\n\nHere is the breakdown of why:\n\n---\n\n### 2. Offer Breakdown & Hidden Realities\n\n#### **Base Salary ($235,000) & Bonus ($47,000)**\n* **Base:** With a posted range of **$183,000 to $271,000**, $235k is around the 60th percentile. There is substantial room to move up toward the top of the band ($250k\u2013$260k).\n* **Bonus:** 20% target is standard for L6 at Google (similar to Meta's 20% target). Note that Meta\u2019s bonus multiplier (company + individual performance) historically pays out more generously than Google\u2019s.\n\n#### **Equity ($1,080,000 over 4 years)**\n* An initial grant of $1.08M ($270k/yr average) is strong for an opening offer, where initial anchors often start around $850k\u2013$950k. However, top-of-band for L6 PM can reach **$1.25M\u2013$1.4M+**.\n* **Google's Vesting Schedule:** Google typically uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (**33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**):\n  * **Year 1:** ~$356,400\n  * **Year 2:** ~$356,400\n  * **Year 3:** ~$237,600 *(significant drop)*\n  * **Year 4:** ~$129,600 *(steep drop)*\n\n#### **The \"Refresher Stacking\" Reality**\nThe recruiter is correct that refreshes stack over their multi-year vesting periods, but:\n1. Refreshes are **discretionary and performance-dependent**.\n2. Because your initial grant drops by ~$119k in Year 3 and another ~$108k in Year 4, your annual refreshes from Years 1 and 2 will largely be **filling that hole** rather than growing your net total compensation until Year 4+.\n3. At Meta, equity vests evenly (25%/yr), making your baseline compensation trajectory much more predictable.\n\n#### **Sign-on Bonus ($0)**\n* Having **$0 sign-on** is a standard opening tactic, but for an external senior hire poached from a top-tier peer, leaving out a sign-on bonus leaves money on the table\u2014especially when you are walking away from unvested Meta RSUs.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou are in the strongest possible negotiating position:\n1. **You were poached:** Google reached out to you.\n2. **You are happy at Meta:** Your BATNA (Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement) is staying in a high-paying, stable role you already like.\n3. **There is zero downside:** Google will not rescind an offer for professional, data-backed negotiation.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For & Exact Targets\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale / Leverage |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Pushes closer to the top of the posted $271k band. |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $1,080,000 / 4 yrs | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | Offsets the Y3/Y4 vesting cliff and matches top-of-band L6 equity. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Bridges the gap for leaving unvested Meta equity and your upcoming bonus cycle. |\n\n*With these adjustments, your **Year 1 Total Compensation** moves from **~$638k** to **~$780k+**.*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nSchedule a call with the recruiter rather than negotiating entirely over email. Frame your position around your contentment at Meta and the financial friction of switching.\n\n#### **Talking Points:**\n1. **Express appreciation:** Validate the team, the scope, and the interview experience.\n2. **Anchor to your current reality:** Emphasize that because you were not actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, the financial delta needs to be compelling enough to justify the transition risk.\n3. **Address the equity structure:** Note that while the Year 1/2 equity is solid, the front-loaded dropoff in Years 3/4 requires a higher overall initial grant to make the 4-year picture work.\n\n#### **Sample Script:**\n> *\"Thank you again for putting this package together. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and the problem space at Google is exciting.*\n> \n> *As you know, I wasn\u2019t actively looking when you reached out, and I\u2019m in a very strong position at Meta with solid momentum and substantial unvested equity. For me to make a lateral move from IC6 to L6, the numbers need to clearly justify walking away from my current trajectory.*\n> \n> *To close this out and get me to sign, here is what would make this an easy 'yes':*\n> - *A base salary of **$255,000**, which reflects senior-level scope within the band.*\n> - *An equity grant of **$1.3M**, which helps balance out the vesting drop in Years 3 and 4.*\n> - *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000\u2013$100,000** to bridge the transition and offset unvested equity I\u2019m leaving behind.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to that level, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and join the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nLet Google make the next move. If they meet your targets or get close (e.g., $250k base + $1.2M equity + $50k sign-on), you have a compelling, top-tier L6 package. If they refuse to budge, you can comfortably stay at Meta knowing you didn't leave value on the table.",
